[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How long should the stored identity survive? → localStorage + logout (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last clarifying question before I put approaches together.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what the shared thing actually holds. "Track who logged in" can mean [3mthe app knows who's currently logged [0m
  [3min[0m, or [3mthe app keeps a record of each login[0m. Those are different data models, and the second is the one that's awkward
  to add later without a migration of whatever's already in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m.

  - [1mCurrent identity only[0m — [38;5;153mget()[39m / [38;5;153mset()[39m / [38;5;153mclear()[39m. One record, overwritten on each login. Everything other forms need
    to attach a user to a submission. Smallest thing that satisfies the request.
  - [1mIdentity + change notification[0m — same, plus other forms can subscribe so they react to login/logout without a page
    reload. Only earns its keep if forms coexist on one page with the login form; across page loads a plain read is
    enough.
  - [1mIdentity + login history[0m — also append each login to a stored list. This is the literal reading of "track who logged
    in." But with no backend, that history lives only in the one browser that produced it, so it can't answer the
    question across users or devices — and it grows unbounded in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m unless the design caps it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m current identity only. The history variant reads like what you asked for, but a per-browser list
  that the user can clear isn't a record you can trust, and I'd rather not build a data structure whose main property is
  looking like analytics without being analytics. If you do want the history, say so and I'll include the cap and
  pruning rules in the spec.
